==Abstract==

This NEP defines a URI-based transport mechanism for application-to-application authentication on NEO.

It allows a requesting application to invoke a wallet application via a custom URI scheme, submit a NEP-20 authentication challenge payload, and receive the authentication result through a callback URI.

This specification is intended exclusively for one-time authentication flows and MUST NOT be used for transaction signing, smart contract invocation, asset transfers, or session-based authorization.

==Motivation==

NEP-20 defines a generic authentication scheme based on digital signatures.

In native environments such as mobile and desktop operating systems, applications typically authenticate users by invoking external wallet applications via URI schemes.

Without a standardized approach, wallets and applications implement incompatible invocation and callback formats, leading to fragmentation.

This NEP standardizes:

* how an application invokes a wallet for authentication;
* how NEP-20 payloads are transported;
* how authentication results are returned;
* how failures are represented using JSON-RPC error objects.

==Specification==

===Overview===

The authentication flow involves:

# the requesting application;
# the wallet application;
# the operating system routing URI invocations.

This specification defines only the transport layer. Authentication semantics are defined by NEP-20.

===Terminology===

====Global authentication scheme====

A standardized URI scheme (<code>neoauth</code>) used to invoke compatible wallet applications.

====Host====

The host component identifies the target wallet namespace.

The reserved host <code>wallet</code> indicates a generic invocation target. In this case, wallet selection is delegated to the operating system or the user.

A wallet-specific host MAY be used to target a particular wallet implementation.

====dApp identifier====

A string identifying the requesting application.

This value MUST be stable and unique within the application's ecosystem.

===Request URI===

The requesting application invokes a wallet using:

<pre>
neoauth://wallet/authenticate?payload=<url_encoded_payload>&dapp=<dapp_identifier>
</pre>

====Parameters====

=====payload=====

A URL-encoded JSON string containing a NEP-20 challenge payload.

The decoded payload MUST conform to NEP-20.

Example:

<pre>
{
  "action": "Authentication",
  "grant_type": "Signature",
  "allowed_algorithms": ["ECDSA-P256"],
  "domain": "mygame.example",
  "networks": [860833102],
  "nonce": "13458238842203010919",
  "timestamp": 1774665600
}
</pre>

=====dapp=====

The identifier of the requesting application.

===Callback URI===

After processing, the wallet invokes:

====Success====

<pre>
dapp://<dapp_identifier>/auth?result=<url_encoded_result>
</pre>

====Failure====

<pre>
dapp://<dapp_identifier>/auth?error=<url_encoded_error>
</pre>

The wallet MUST construct the callback URI using the <code>dapp</code> parameter from the request URI.

The <code>callback</code> field defined in NEP-20 MUST be ignored in this transport mode.

The callback URI MUST contain exactly one of <code>result</code> or <code>error</code>.

===Successful Result===

The <code>result</code> parameter is a URL-encoded JSON string.

The decoded JSON MUST be the NEP-20 authentication response payload.

Example:

<pre>
{
  "algorithm": "ECDSA-P256",
  "network": 860833102,
  "pubkey": "0355912bc4e61c9715c5912397ea53a5ac6c103c4893fbd9c2a9f3be13b7a3e29d",
  "address": "NfMFWYxaUUQy9SYo6AhRiGmRxfPxe9Edj7",
  "nonce": "13458238842203010919",
  "timestamp": 1774665601,
  "signature": "BAS7Ljufj3vrhOrTAi21D/5Cf62n4r64Suf/do8dq/OCMHiLJl+hLJeMFZwTajVjhcpFLz6FuSEp13vvEqWf1w=="
}
</pre>

The requesting application or its backend MUST verify this payload according to NEP-20.

===Error Result===

The <code>error</code> parameter is a URL-encoded JSON string.

The decoded JSON MUST follow the JSON-RPC error object format:

<pre>
{
  "code": -32000,
  "message": "User cancelled the authentication request.",
  "data": null
}
</pre>

The <code>data</code> field is optional.

===Processing Rules===

====Requesting application====

The requesting application:

# constructs a NEP-20 challenge payload;
# URL-encodes it;
# invokes the request URI;
# waits for callback;
# if <code>result</code> is returned, verifies it according to NEP-20;
# if <code>error</code> is returned, treats authentication as failed.

The requesting application MUST NOT treat the callback URI itself as proof of authentication.

====Wallet application====

The wallet MUST:

# validate the request URI format;
# decode and validate the payload;
# present authentication details to the user, including the domain;
# require explicit user approval;
# if approved:
## generate NEP-20 response payload;
## invoke callback with <code>result</code>;
# if rejected or failed:
## invoke callback with <code>error</code>.

If callback invocation is not possible, the wallet may report the failure locally, but this does not constitute a valid protocol response.

===Examples===

====Request====

<pre>
neoauth://wallet/authenticate?payload=%7B%22action%22%3A%22Authentication%22%2C%22grant_type%22%3A%22Signature%22%2C%22allowed_algorithms%22%3A%5B%22ECDSA-P256%22%5D%2C%22domain%22%3A%22mygame.example%22%2C%22networks%22%3A%5B860833102%5D%2C%22nonce%22%3A%2213458238842203010919%22%2C%22timestamp%22%3A1774665600%7D&dapp=mygame
</pre>

====Success====

<pre>
dapp://mygame/auth?result=%7B%22algorithm%22%3A%22ECDSA-P256%22%2C%22network%22%3A860833102%2C%22pubkey%22%3A%220355912bc4e61c9715c5912397ea53a5ac6c103c4893fbd9c2a9f3be13b7a3e29d%22%2C%22address%22%3A%22NfMFWYxaUUQy9SYo6AhRiGmRxfPxe9Edj7%22%2C%22nonce%22%3A%2213458238842203010919%22%2C%22timestamp%22%3A1774665601%2C%22signature%22%3A%22BAS7Ljufj3vrhOrTAi21D%2F5Cf62n4r64Suf%2Fdo8dq%2FOCMHiLJl%2BhLJeMFZwTajVjhcpFLz6FuSEp13vvEqWf1w%3D%3D%22%7D
</pre>

====Failure====

<pre>
dapp://mygame/auth?error=%7B%22code%22%3A-32001%2C%22message%22%3A%22User%20cancelled%22%7D
</pre>

===Security Considerations===

====Trust Model====

This specification uses custom URI schemes, which are not secure transport channels.

It does not guarantee confidentiality, integrity, or application identity.

Security relies entirely on NEP-20 signature verification.

====Authentication Security====

Authentication MUST be established exclusively through NEP-20 verification.

The callback URI MUST NOT be trusted as proof of authentication.

====Scope Limitation====

This specification MUST NOT be used for:

* transaction signing;
* asset transfers;
* smart contract invocation;
* session-based authorization.

====Callback Interception====

Callback URIs may be intercepted.

Applications MUST treat callback data as publicly observable.

====Request Forgery====

Wallets MUST NOT trust request origin and MUST require explicit user approval.

====Phishing====

Wallets MUST clearly display the domain field.

====Replay Protection====

Nonces MUST be unique and single-use.

Expiration SHOULD be enforced (recommended: 5 minutes).

====Data Exposure====

Payloads MUST NOT contain secrets.

====Wallet Identity====

The host component is not a secure identity guarantee.

===Compatibility===

Fully compatible with NEP-20.

===Rationale===

This design:

* reuses NEP-20;
* avoids redefining authentication formats;
* introduces a global authentication entry point;
* allows wallet selection to be delegated to the operating system;
* keeps scope minimal and focused.
